      },
      "worked": "Let the frequency in the 0–2 hours class be $x$. The 2–4 hours class then has frequency $2x$. If $3x$ were at least $12$, both middle study times would be in the first two classes, giving a median at most $4$ hours. Since $3x$ is a multiple of $3$, it is at most $9$, so $x\\leq 3$.\n\nIf $x\\leq 2$, the highest frequency is at most $4$. Five distinct non-negative frequencies at most $4$ have a total of at most $0+1+2+3+4=10$. Hence $x=3$, and the first two frequencies are $3$ and $6$.\n\nLet $y$ be the frequency in the 8–10 hours class. The 6–8 hours class has frequency $2y$. The last two frequencies are distinct and both are less than $6$, so $y$ is either $1$ or $2$. If $y=1$, the middle class would need $20-3-6-2-1=8$ students, exceeding the highest frequency of $6$. Thus $y=2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f_{4\\text{–}6} &= 20-3-6-4-2\\\\f_{4\\text{–}6} &= 5.\\end{align*}$$\nThe frequencies in order are $3,6,5,4,2$. The 10th and 11th observations fall in the 4–6 hours class, which can have a median of $5$ hours. The given mean above the median suggests positive skew.",
